<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali Iqbal al-Dawla</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali Iqbal al-Dawla was the ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denia">Dénia</a> from 1044 to 1076, son and successor of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mujahid-al-amiri">Mujahid al-Amiri</a>. Captured as a boy during his father's Sardinian expedition of 1015–1016, he was held for years by the Christians before returning. He ruled a maritime state that included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a> and kept close ties with Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, until al-Muqtadir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> deposed him and annexed Dénia in 1076.</p></div>
